 ## Overview
-The Monte Carlo Specular Energy Aggregator (MC-SEA) is a compression technique that maps high-order (third-order) Spherical Harmonics (SH) radiance energy into a compact lower-order (first-order) subspace without pre-training or distillation [S1]. It solves the problem of high memory overhead and inference cost associated with third-order SH in mobile rendering by extracting directional moments of the specular residual via Monte Carlo projection [S1]. Unlike Mobile-GS, which relies on costly teacher-student distillation, MC-SEA preserves high-frequency specular features by aggregating photometric residuals into a latent space, allowing the scene to be rendered with lightweight first-order SH parameters [S1].
+The Monte Carlo Specular Energy Aggregator (MC-SEA) is a compression technique used in 3D Gaussian Splatting to map high-order (e.g., third-order) Spherical Harmonics (SH) radiance energy into a compact lower-order subspace (e.g., first-order) without pre-training or distillation [S1]. It solves the problem of high memory overhead and inference cost associated with high-order SH by extracting directional moments of the specular residual via Monte Carlo projection, preserving magnitude and spatial direction [S1]. Unlike Mobile-GS, which relies on expensive teacher-student distillation, MC-SEA compresses energy directly, improving training efficiency while maintaining competitive PSNR [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
